SimpleLdapAuthenticator
=======================

Allows for simple authentication to an LDAP server with a minimum of
configuration.  Requires either Ruby/LDAP or Net::LDAP.   

Example Usage:

  require 'simple_ldap_authenticator'
  require 'logger'

  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.servers = %w'dc1.domain.com dc2.domain.com'
  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.use_ssl = true
  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.login_format = '%s@domain.com'
  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.logger = Logger.new($stdout)

  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.valid?(username, password)
  # => true or false (or raise if there is an issue connecting to the server)

By default, a new connection object is created for each call to valid?,
so valid? is safe to call concurrently from multiple threads.  If you only
use SimpleLdapAuthenticator from a single thread, you can reuse a single
connection object for all calls to valid?:

  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.single_threaded = true

Do not enable single threaded mode if valid? may be called concurrently,
as that can result in invalid passwords being considered valid.

When use_ssl is true, the server's certificate and hostname are verified
using the default trusted certificates.  You can set use_ssl to a hash of
TLS options to customize this, such as specifying the CA certificate(s) to
trust (useful for internal CAs and self-signed certificates, such as those
commonly used by Active Directory):

  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.use_ssl = {ca_file: '/path/to/ca.pem'}

The :ca_file, :ca_path, and :verify_mode options are supported for both
ldap and net/ldap backends.  For net/ldap, any option supported by
OpenSSL::SSL::SSLContext#set_params can be used.  For ldap, only
those three options are supported. :verify_mode on ldap will default to
the LDAP behavior (which verifies by default unless the TLS_REQCERT
setting is set in the system's ldap.conf).

Before version 2, use_ssl = true did not verify the server's
certificate when using net/ldap.  To get the previous behavior of not
verifying the server's certificate (not recommended, as it allows
man-in-the-middle attacks to capture passwords):

  SimpleLdapAuthenticator.use_ssl = {verify_mode: OpenSSL::SSL::VERIFY_NONE}

Note that due to a bug in net/ldap, you should use domain names and
not IP addresses in the servers array, at least if you are using
LibreSSL (which follows the SSL spec more closely).

github: http://github.com/jeremyevans/simple_ldap_authenticator
